# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# RDR-ART: an entry that has a picture - the caption, the drawing after
# it, and the screen underneath given back whole (#328).
# The picture is the page after the caption, so `NEXT` walks onto it. It
# is drawn by plane surgery into the box the program's own frame drawer
# has just put up, and in the arrival *after* the one that queued that
# frame: a handler's own pixels land the instant it runs and a call into
# the program lands when the batch does, so a picture drawn beside the
# frame goes under it.
# A single leg, for rdr-bar.leg's reason: `N` and `E` reach the program
# in a seam-off run, so a pair would be diffing the program's own answer
# to two letters as well as the reader.
# Driven by hand on the shorter boot (`--code-wheel-answered`, #291),
# which the runner cannot yet drive; #293 owes every leg its re-drive.

about a picture of an entry: the caption, the drawing after it, and the screen given back
kind single
program START.EXE

# A store of its own, so that the four legs which fill the log with
# --cite-all-journal and open whatever row the cursor lands on keep the
# frames they pin. The picture is this repository's own and is not a
# drawing of anything (PLAN.md §6): 152x120, half the box so the centring
# shows, and the four things a reader of one can get wrong side by side -
# rows 0..39, four bars of thirty-eight pixels, one per stored tone,
# darkest first; rows 40..79, a one-pixel comb of ink and paper in
# alternate columns, which is what the panel's 2x1 average exists for,
# since a sampler would throw every other line away; rows 80..119, a solid
# block of ink at x 56..95; and a one-pixel ink border round the whole of
# it. `art_ramp` in `seam_journal.cpp` is which palette index each tone
# becomes.
store tests/visual/reader-art-store.txt
both code-wheel

# The boot #291 left, as rdr-bar.leg takes it: the seam on and the challenge
# already answered, so there is no wheel screen in this script.
code-wheel-answered
seam journal
until 300000000
every 25

# The prompt rather than the log, because a picture is a fact about an entry
# and not about what the game has cited: F1, the number, Return, `NEXT`
# onto the drawing, and `EXIT` out through the program's own composer.
key L@7550
key A@7800
key F1@9600
key 4@10000
key Return@10400
key N@11600
key E@13000

# Each screen, holding still with nothing pressed. Measured off the stills
# at 25 frames: the prompt at 9,625, the digit at 10,025, the entry's text
# page - full screen, because it was opened from the party's own bar - from
# 10,575, the picture from 11,675, and the adventuring screen again from
# 13,200. A picture page settles in about 75 frames where a twenty-row text
# page takes about 230 (#305): one batch for the clear, the frame and the
# bar, and then one arrival more, which for a picture is a single blit.
same 9700-9975
same 10100-10375
same 10600-11575
same 11700-12975

# And the screen the journal was opened over, back again after it with
# nothing excepted - the same `equal` rdr-bar.leg earned when the `Notes`
# splice began handing the bar highlight back (#330). Nothing here touches
# that bar, so 13,200 onwards is the 9,000-9,600 still exactly.
same 13250-15075
equal 9575 13500
